Subject: [PATCH] gl: serve the profile's driver strings from the binding layer

GL_RENDERER and GL_VENDOR have two independent consumers, and patching either
one alone leaves the other describing the host's real hardware.

GPUInfo reads them with a direct glGetString in the GPU process, which drives
chrome://gpu and — more importantly — the driver-bug blocklist, whose decisions
are keyed on exactly these strings. WebGL reads them through the command buffer
decoder, and that is what UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL returns to a page. A build
that claims one GPU to JavaScript while making blocklist decisions for another
is contradicting itself in a way that shows up as a different feature set, not
just a different string.

RealGLApi::glGetStringFn is the single point both paths pass through, and it
already does this for GL_EXTENSIONS: the filtered-extension substitution
immediately above is the same shape. So the two consumers agree by construction
rather than by keeping two patches in sync.

Deliberately not touched: WebGL's *masked* GL_RENDERER, which Blink answers with
the constant "WebKit WebGL" whatever the hardware. Conflating the masked and
unmasked paths would introduce a difference from stock Chrome rather than remove
one.

The substituted strings live in function-local statics because this returns a
borrowed pointer that must outlive the call, and the profile is immutable for
process lifetime.

Ledger: webgl.unmasked-renderer, webgl.unmasked-vendor, gpuinfo.gl-renderer,
coh.gpu-info-vs-webgl.

diff --git a/base/apostate/profile.cc b/base/apostate/profile.cc
index 2d479a8..8a64885 100644
--- a/base/apostate/profile.cc
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.cc
@@ -104,6 +104,15 @@ Profile* Profile::ParseOrNull(const std::string& encoded) {
     }
   }
 
+  if (const DictValue* gpu = dict->FindDict("gpu")) {
+    if (const std::string* renderer = gpu->FindString("unmasked_renderer")) {
+      profile->gpu_renderer_ = *renderer;
+    }
+    if (const std::string* vendor = gpu->FindString("unmasked_vendor")) {
+      profile->gpu_vendor_ = *vendor;
+    }
+  }
+
   return profile.release();
 }
 
diff --git a/base/apostate/profile.h b/base/apostate/profile.h
index ff33d3d..0776033 100644
--- a/base/apostate/profile.h
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.h
@@ -70,6 +70,22 @@ class BASE_EXPORT Profile {
     return memory_total_bytes_;
   }
 
+  // GL_RENDERER and GL_VENDOR as the claimed device's driver reports them.
+  //
+  // These are consumed at the GL binding layer rather than in Blink, because
+  // two independent consumers read them — GPUInfo in the GPU process, which
+  // drives chrome://gpu and the blocklist, and WebGL's
+  // UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL, which is what a page actually sees. Overriding
+  // either one alone leaves the other describing the host's real hardware.
+  //
+  // Note this does not touch WebGL's *masked* GL_RENDERER, which Blink answers
+  // with the constant "WebKit WebGL" regardless of hardware. Conflating the two
+  // would introduce a difference from stock Chrome rather than remove one.
+  const std::optional<std::string>& gpu_renderer() const {
+    return gpu_renderer_;
+  }
+  const std::optional<std::string>& gpu_vendor() const { return gpu_vendor_; }
+
   // Identifier of the capture this profile was derived from, for diagnostics.
   const std::string& source_id() const { return source_id_; }
 
@@ -78,6 +94,8 @@ class BASE_EXPORT Profile {
 
   std::optional<int> cpu_logical_cores_;
   std::optional<uint64_t> memory_total_bytes_;
+  std::optional<std::string> gpu_renderer_;
+  std::optional<std::string> gpu_vendor_;
   std::string source_id_;
 };
 
diff --git a/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc b/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
index 6e785fa..3ebdfb1 100644
--- a/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.cc
@@ -4,6 +4,9 @@
 
 #include "ui/gl/gl_gl_api_implementation.h"
 
+#include "base/apostate/profile.h"
+#include "base/no_destructor.h"
+
 #include <array>
 #include <vector>
 
@@ -247,6 +250,36 @@ const GLubyte* RealGLApi::glGetStringFn(GLenum name) {
     InitializeFilteredExtensionsIfNeeded();
     return reinterpret_cast<const GLubyte*>(filtered_exts_str_.c_str());
   }
+
+  // Apostate: substitute the profile's driver strings here, at the binding
+  // layer, because this is the one point both consumers pass through.
+  //
+  // GPUInfo reads them via glGetString directly in the GPU process, driving
+  // chrome://gpu and the blocklist; WebGL reads them through the command
+  // buffer decoder, which is what UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL returns to a page.
+  // Patching either site alone leaves the other describing the host's real
+  // hardware, and a claimed GPU whose blocklist decisions were made for a
+  // different one is its own contradiction.
+  //
+  // The strings are held in function-local statics because this returns a
+  // borrowed pointer that must outlive the call, and the profile is immutable
+  // for process lifetime.
+  if (name == GL_RENDERER || name == GL_VENDOR) {
+    if (const base::apostate::Profile* profile =
+            base::apostate::Profile::Get()) {
+      if (name == GL_RENDERER && profile->gpu_renderer().has_value()) {
+        static const base::NoDestructor<std::string> renderer(
+            *profile->gpu_renderer());
+        return reinterpret_cast<const GLubyte*>(renderer->c_str());
+      }
+      if (name == GL_VENDOR && profile->gpu_vendor().has_value()) {
+        static const base::NoDestructor<std::string> vendor(
+            *profile->gpu_vendor());
+        return reinterpret_cast<const GLubyte*>(vendor->c_str());
+      }
+    }
+  }
+
   return GLApiBase::glGetStringFn(name);
 }
 
